{"id": "wd-Q11531476", "status": "ok", "meta": "Hasegawa Tōhaku's ink screens of pine trees in mist, painted around 1595 and now a National Treasure in the Tokyo National Museum.", "about": "Pine trunks rise out of mist, some sharp and some almost gone. Hasegawa Tōhaku painted them in ink on paper for a pair of folding screens with six panels each. The exact date is not known. Around 1595 is the estimate, in the Momoyama period. This is the left screen. Wikipedia says they are thought to be the first paintings of this size with trees as the only subject, and a pale shape at upper right might be a distant mountain. The screens are in the Tokyo National Museum and were named a National Treasure of Japan in 1952.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHasegawa_Tohaku_-_Pine_Trees_(Sh%C5%8Drin-zu_by%C5%8Dbu)_-_left_hand_screen.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHasegawa_Tohaku_-_Pine_Trees_(Sh%C5%8Drin-zu_by%C5%8Dbu)_-_left_hand_screen.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shōrin-zu_byōbu"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "wd-Q119139271", "status": "ok", "meta": "John Lavery's 1884 painting of a laundress chatting with a boatman on the River Loing, now in the Ulster Museum in Belfast.", "about": "A laundress pauses in her work to talk with a boatman passing on the river. A child sits in a cart beside her, and a cherry tree leans over the water with a stone bridge beyond. John Lavery painted it in 1884, after a visit to the artists' colony at Grez-sur-Loing while he was studying in Paris. It was one of his most ambitious early paintings. It has also been called On the Loing, an Afternoon Chat. The Ulster Museum in Belfast owns it.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASir_John_Lavery_%E2%80%93_Under_the_Cherry_Tree.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ASir_John_Lavery_%E2%80%93_Under_the_Cherry_Tree.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_the_Cherry_Tree"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "wd-Q1212861", "status": "ok", "meta": "Alexandre Cabanel's The Birth of Venus, shown at the 1863 Paris Salon and bought by Napoleon III, now in the Musée d'Orsay.", "about": "A nude Venus lies on the crest of a wave with her eyes closed, while winged cherubs hover in the sky above her. Alexandre Cabanel showed the painting at the Paris Salon of 1863, and Napoleon III bought it for his own collection at once. Cabanel was made a professor at the École des Beaux-Arts that same year. He later made two more versions, and the third, from 1875, is in the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The Musée d'Orsay has the first. Robert Rosenblum wrote that her eyes seem closed but are in fact awake.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlexandre_Cabanel_-_The_Birth_of_Venus_-_Google_Art_Project_2.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlexandre_Cabanel_-_The_Birth_of_Venus_-_Google_Art_Project_2.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Birth_of_Venus_(Cabanel)"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "wd-Q153788", "status": "ok", "meta": "Rembrandt's painting of Diana with her nymphs, joining the stories of Actaeon and Callisto from Ovid, at Anholt Castle in Germany.", "about": "Two stories from Ovid share one forest clearing. On the left, Actaeon sees Diana naked, is turned into a stag and is killed by his own hounds. On the right, the nymphs pull the clothes off Callisto to reveal that she has broken her vow of chastity and is carrying Jupiter's child. Diana drives her out. Rembrandt also added an elderly couple in the background who belong to neither story, and a middle-aged nymph in the foreground. Wikipedia dates the painting to 1634. It belongs to the Salm-Salm princely collection at Anholt.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARembrandt%2C_Harmenszoon_van_Rijn_-_Diana_mit_Akt%C3%A4on_und_Kallisto_-_c.1634-1635.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARembrandt%2C_Harmenszoon_van_Rijn_-_Diana_mit_Akt%C3%A4on_und_Kallisto_-_c.1634-1635.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Bathing_with_her_Nymphs_with_Actaeon_and_Callisto"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "wd-Q17319701", "status": "ok", "meta": "Ferdinand Bol's 1667 portrait of Michiel de Ruyter as lieutenant-admiral, one of six made for the Dutch Admiralty offices, now in the Rijksmuseum.", "about": "Michiel de Ruyter stands with one arm on a globe, a rolled document in his hand and a chart on the table beside him. Behind him lies a seascape. Ferdinand Bol painted him in 1667 in memory of the Four Days Battle. Six identical versions were made, each hanging in one of the offices of the Dutch Republic's Admiralty. This one was meant for the Admiralty of Zeeland in Middelburg, and de Ruyter himself gave it. The sea scene is thought to be by Willem van de Velde the Younger, and it includes de Ruyter's flagship De Zeven Provinciën.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_Michiel_de_Ruyter_by_Ferdinand_Bol_Rijksmuseum_Amsterdam_SK-A-44.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_Michiel_de_Ruyter_by_Ferdinand_Bol_Rijksmuseum_Amsterdam_SK-A-44.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Michiel_de_Ruyter"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "wd-Q19924921", "status": "ok", "meta": "John Trumbull's full-length 1792 portrait of Alexander Hamilton, donated in 2013 to the Metropolitan Museum of Art and Crystal Bridges.", "about": "Alexander Hamilton stands in a gray coat with powdered hair, one hand on a green-draped table that holds inkwells, a quill and a sheet of paper. An arched opening is behind him. John Trumbull painted the portrait in 1792, and it is one of several he made of Hamilton. In 2013 Credit Suisse donated it to both the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York and the Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art in Arkansas. The Met calls it the greatest known portrait of Hamilton and one of the finest civic portraits from the Federal period.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoogle_Arts_Project_-_Alexander_Hamilton_(1792_portrait)_by_John_Trumbull.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoogle_Arts_Project_-_Alexander_Hamilton_(1792_portrait)_by_John_Trumbull.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Hamilton_(Trumbull)"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "wd-Q19857229", "status": "ok", "meta": "Jan van Eyck's small panel of the Virgin and Child with saints and the donor Jan Vos, begun in 1441 and finished by his workshop.", "about": "The Virgin holds the Christ Child in a blue mantle before a red cloth, with a kneeling donor in white on one side and saints around them. The donor is Jan Vos, who took office as prior of a Carthusian monastery near Bruges in March 1441 and commissioned the panel. Van Eyck died in 1442 before it was done. Art historians generally agree that he painted the central Madonna and Child and planned the whole design. A workshop member finished the saints and the background, borrowing freely from earlier van Eyck paintings. It is thought to be his last work. The Frick Collection bought it in 1954.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_van_Eyck_-_Virgin_and_Child%2C_with_Saints_and_Donor_-_1441_-_Frick_Collection.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_van_Eyck_-_Virgin_and_Child%2C_with_Saints_and_Donor_-_1441_-_Frick_Collection.jpg", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madonna_of_Jan_Vos"], "check": "clean"}
